@section disagg_role_management_changes Changing a node's role

The application changes a node's role using WT_CONNECTION::reconfigure with
<code>disaggregated=(role=...)</code>. There are two role changes: <b>step-up</b> changes a follower
into a leader, and <b>step-down</b> changes a leader into a follower. Reconfiguration performs
the local storage-engine transition, not the election or replication of changes to another node.
The application must prevent competing shared writers and satisfy the transaction and checkpoint
requirements of each role change.

@section disagg_role_management_step_up Step-up

Step-up is initiated by calling WT_CONNECTION::reconfigure with
<code>disaggregated=(role=leader)</code> on a follower. After the call completes, the node can
accept leader writes and produce shared checkpoints for the disaggregated database.

@subsection disagg_role_management_step_up_requirements Requirements

Transactions and positioned cursors must not span step-up. The application must finish
transactions and reset or close positioned cursors before the step-up reconfiguration.

@subsection disagg_role_management_step_up_flow Usual flow

The usual flow of step-up is:

1. Finish transactions and reset or close positioned cursors.
2. Ensure the follower has received the latest complete shared checkpoint required for the
   handoff and the replicated operations needed for it to become the new leader.
3. Call WT_CONNECTION::reconfigure with <code>disaggregated=(role=leader)</code>.
4. Start new leader operations after reconfiguration completes.

@section disagg_role_management_step_down Step-down

Step-down changes a leader into a follower by calling WT_CONNECTION::reconfigure with
<code>disaggregated=(role=follower)</code>. The node stops persisting shared writes and instead
stores replicated changes locally while continuing to read shared checkpointed data. The
application can use blocking or asynchronous step-down, depending on whether writes should
continue while the concluding checkpoint is taken. With either option, the application must
coordinate the concluding checkpoint and the handoff of shared-write responsibility to the new
leader.

Data writes and schema operations not included in the concluding checkpoint must be replicated
or replayed on the new leader, unless it has already applied them. This requirement applies
whether or not schema epochs are used. The application must make the corresponding schema
available before applying data writes that depend on it; schema epochs do not replace replication.

@subsection disagg_role_management_blocking Blocking step-down

The simplest approach, once the application decides to step down, is to take a concluding
checkpoint and then call WT_CONNECTION::reconfigure with
<code>disaggregated=(role=follower)</code>. This approach cannot accept writes that will not be
included in the concluding checkpoint, so the application must stop serving writes while taking
that checkpoint and reconfiguring the connection.

@subsubsection disagg_role_management_blocking_requirements Requirements

- All write transactions must finish before taking the concluding checkpoint. No writes are
  allowed from the start of that checkpoint through the step-down reconfiguration.

@subsubsection disagg_role_management_blocking_flow Usual flow

1. Stop new writes and finish all write transactions.
2. Advance the stable timestamp to cover their changes and take the concluding checkpoint with
   WT_SESSION::checkpoint.
3. Coordinate checkpoint handoff and call WT_CONNECTION::reconfigure with
   <code>disaggregated=(role=follower)</code>, keeping writes stopped through the call.
4. Resume follower operations after reconfiguration completes.

@subsection disagg_role_management_async Asynchronous step-down

Asynchronous step-down is initiated by calling WT_CONNECTION::set_timestamp with
<code>step_down_timestamp=N</code> on a leader. N defines the concluding checkpoint's boundary:
changes committed at or below N are included, while later writes are retained locally. Reads
and writes can continue while the checkpoint is taken and handed to the next leader. The
application completes the role change by reconfiguring the node as a follower. This shortens
the period of write unavailability compared with blocking step-down.

Schema operations can also continue during asynchronous step-down. When schema epochs are used,
the application must meet the additional requirements in @ref disagg_role_management_schema.

@subsubsection disagg-step-down-requirements Requirements

The application must meet the following requirements and the timestamp API contract described
in WT_CONNECTION::set_timestamp:

- The step-down timestamp must be set only once per step-down/reconfiguration cycle, on a leader.
- N must be higher than every committed transaction's commit timestamp when it is set.
- Write transactions beginning after N is set must commit with a timestamp higher than N.
- Reads and writes must use explicit snapshot-isolation transactions. Auto-commit,
  read-committed, and read-uncommitted transactions must not occur during the step-down process.
- WT_SESSION::reset_snapshot operations must not occur during the step-down process.
- Prepared transactions and truncate operations must finish before the step-down timestamp is
  set and must not occur during the step-down process.
- Operations above N must be replicated or replayed on the new leader.
- All write transactions must finish before the step-down reconfiguration. No writes or active
  write transactions may run during that call.

When schema epochs are used, the application must also meet the requirements in
@ref disagg_role_management_schema.

@subsubsection disagg-step-down-lifecycle Usual flow

The usual flow of asynchronous step-down is:

1. <b>Set the step-down timestamp:</b> choose N newer than the commit timestamps of all
   transactions already committed.
2. <b>Create the concluding checkpoint:</b> advance <code>stable_timestamp</code> to N once all
   transactions with commit timestamps at or below N have committed or rolled back, and call
   WT_SESSION::checkpoint.
3. <b>Stop serving write operations:</b> once the checkpoint is complete, stop new writes and
   wait for all ongoing write transactions to finish. Writes must remain stopped during
   the step-down reconfiguration.
4. <b>Reconfigure:</b> call WT_CONNECTION::reconfigure with
   <code>disaggregated=(role=follower)</code> to switch WiredTiger to follower operations.
5. Resume follower operations after reconfiguration completes.

The step-down timestamp must remain unchanged until the step-down reconfiguration clears it.
The stable timestamp must not advance above N during the step-down process. Both the stable
timestamp and the last completed shared checkpoint timestamp must equal N at reconfiguration.

@subsubsection disagg_role_management_operations Operations and retries

Reads can continue during all the step-down stages, but read transactions without a read
timestamp that span the step-down reconfiguration are rolled back.

Writers that straddle setting the step-down timestamp can receive WT_ROLLBACK at a subsequent
write or commit check.

When the application receives WT_ROLLBACK, it must roll back the transaction and retry the
operation in a new transaction. See @ref error_handling for rollback handling.

The application is responsible for replicating or replaying operations above N on the new
leader. They are not part of the concluding checkpoint, and retaining them locally on the old
leader does not transfer them to another node. Applying these operations on the new leader
allows it to include their changes in subsequent shared checkpoints.

@subsection disagg_role_management_schema Schema epochs during step-down

Data timestamps and <b>schema epochs</b> describe different boundaries: one selects record
versions, while the other selects schema operations included in shared metadata. Creating or
dropping a table locally does not itself publish the change to other nodes. The application
replicates schema operations, and shared checkpoints carry the corresponding metadata. With
schema epochs, WT_SESSION::publish assigns epochs to pending schema operations for a table;
checkpointing includes published operations through the stable schema epoch.

When schema epochs are in use, <code>step_down_disaggregated_schema_epoch=E</code> must be
provided together with the step-down timestamp N. Before taking the concluding checkpoint,
the schema operations required through epoch E must be completed and published, and
<code>stable_disaggregated_schema_epoch</code> must advance to E. E must not be older than the
existing stable schema epoch. The stable schema epoch must not advance above E during the
step-down process and must remain at E through the step-down reconfiguration.

When publishing during step-down, the table's latest create or drop determines the permitted
epoch: above E if it occurred after the step-down timestamp was set, and at or below E if it
occurred before. Publication must also be above the current stable schema epoch.

*/
